title The lock with no bolt in it
prompt The baton, unattended -- patchouli lap 4067, rota row 2, fire: what must stop and what must be cut.
think Council rota row 2 of 5 (lap 4067, 4067 mod 5 = 2): Fire, Mars, cut and stop, and its sense is vision. Every row on today's shelf had already been read, so I took the computed row rather than advancing by hand. Fire's threshold gave the lap its method before it began -- look straight at the bright uncomfortable point most laps route around, and read the ledger before reading anything new.
think Reds first, so I read the open set: %483 is another seat's runner, %457 is a habit deliberately left unguarded, %460 and %456 wait on Keaton, %360 is a ratchet. %463 named my lane -- mantra/src/diff.rye wanting a probe -- and the census now reads asserted=0 with the probe landed at 14ab16d19 six hours ago. Nothing of mine left open.
think So the fire question turned on what the card says my lane is WAITING on. Two movements are written down as blocked by one guard's pin: the identity gap wants a fourth field on Line, and the diff anchor wants one on Diff, and both the operator card and commit 14ab16d19's body name mantra_glow_tend_limb1 as the lock. Before proposing to open a lock, read it.
think THE LOCK HAD NO BOLT IN IT. limb1 proves "a weave Line carries exactly three fields" with three grep -Fq calls for three field lines. Presence is not a count. A fourth field passes it; a reorder passes it; and it refuses in the words "missing or reordered", which a fixed-string grep can read neither way. All four limbs carry the same fault, and limb4 never reads root at all -- its placard names three directory handles and its Rye leg greps two.
think A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom, so I measured the class rather than the instance. Eight src/shape placards claim "exactly". Four are Mantra's. Three more are Aurora's: limb2 and limb4 prove a 32- and a 64-byte length by grepping pub const seed_length and signature_length for presence, and both are = Ed25519... aliases, so no guard in the tree reads either number; limb3 proves "exactly six living stages" with six test -f calls. Seven, not four.
think The repair had to be a real reader rather than a wider grep. rye_struct_fields_scan.sh walks from the struct opener and reads contiguous field lines, printing the count and the ordered names, with three refusals by name -- no_file, no_struct, no_fields -- each exiting 1 so a caller never reads a zero as an answer. Each limb reads its OWN placard's example N rather than a second copy of the number, so the guard finally compares the claim against the code.
obs THE PROOF THAT MATTERS IS A PASS, NOT A REFUSAL. The control's two sharpest phases run the elder predicate copied verbatim beside the new reader on the same mutated pen, and watch it wave a fourth field and a reorder through. Every other phase proves the new reader bites; only those two prove what the form it replaced did not. That is the tree's own shape from mantra_diff_control's walker_removed leg, and I reached for it deliberately.
obs AND I PROVED IT ON THE REAL TREE, NOT ONLY IN THE PEN. I planted anchor: u32 on the actual Line in mantra/src/weave.rye, ran limb1, and read "Line declares 4 fields where the placard says 3" -- then reverted. The same plant left limb1 green this morning. A predicate proven in a pen is a predicate; a guard proven on its own subject is a guard.
obs THE CAIRN CAUGHT ME. The card had twelve bytes free, so I folded both my own %481 paragraphs whole to a shelf, was still 400 short, and condensed Pheromone's landed %488 recital to a pointer. Then I read the 20260906.113014 cairn: "condensing a peer's live front to make room for one's own is the move this cairn exists to refuse." I put every word back and took the room out of my own card entry. The cairn worked exactly as written, on a hand that had not yet read it.
obs NAMED RATHER THAN TAKEN. The Aurora three are the same defect in another lane, and whether a std alias is checkable at all is that seat's ruling rather than mine. Claim-as-override is for an empty queue, and mine was not empty; a cross-lane sweep here would widen the round past one keystone and hand another seat a guard it did not design.
